esp32s31: keep the HID interrupt URB alive, and stop guessing about coherency

The USB keyboard on this board loses keystrokes and sticks keys down: the
kernel autorepeats a key nobody is holding, hundreds of times a second, until
something clears it. Established by measurement, in this order:

  - It is NOT the desktop. It reproduces at the bare fbcon prompt with lvdesk
    not running at all - you cannot log in as root.
  - It is NOT descriptor DMA. Turning it off (the new desc_dma toggle) changed
    nothing: 9 presses, 8 releases, 1229 autorepeats with it disabled.
  - It is NOT descriptor coherency either - see below.
  - It is NOT the hub or its power budget: it reproduces with the receiver
    plugged straight into the board.
  - The kernel is not dropping events. keylog shows every MSC_SCAN paired with
    an EV_KEY, and a good run gives 153 presses / 152 releases / 0 repeats.

The clue that pointed here: presses and releases keep ARRIVING while the repeat
storm runs. HID keyboard reports carry absolute state, so any single report
showing the key released would clear it. A key stuck for hundreds of repeats
therefore means the report that would have cleared it never got collected.

**usbhid.quirks=0x2dc8:0x5201:0x00000400** is HID_QUIRK_ALWAYS_POLL for the
8BitDo receiver: keep the interrupt URB permanently resubmitted instead of
letting it lapse when the device goes quiet. It exists in mainline for exactly
this class of wireless receiver. Under test and looking good, but the fault was
always intermittent so this is not yet called proven.

**dma-noncoherent on the USB node did NOT fix it, and is kept only because it
is independently correct.** riscv selects ARCH_DMA_DEFAULT_COHERENT, so with
neither property present of_dma_is_coherent() returns true and dwc2's
descriptor lists came from an ordinary CACHED linear mapping rather than the
DMA_DIRECT_REMAP path. Whether this SoC actually needs that is UNVERIFIED - the
claim "dma_alloc_coherent returns cached memory here" is repeated throughout
this project's notes and nobody has ever measured it. Evidence against it: with
every device defaulting to coherent, Linux performs zero DMA cache maintenance
on this board - nothing registers riscv_nonstd_cache_ops and this hart has no
zicbom, so arch_sync_dma_for_device() patches to a nop - and yet the SD card
sustains 12 MB/s without corruption. Streaming maps are unaffected here for the
same reason; only the coherent allocation changed.

**desc_dma** is a runtime toggle for descriptor DMA, so the next person can A/B
it in a reboot rather than a rebuild, and dwc2 now logs which side of the
coherency question it landed on at probe.

--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/params.c
@@ -76,10 +76,26 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(host_full_speed,
 		 "Pin the host port to full speed so hubs avoid split transactions");
 
+static bool desc_dma = true;
+module_param(desc_dma, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(desc_dma,
+		 "use descriptor DMA for periodic transfers (default on); "
+		 "off costs CPU but tests the cached-coherent-memory theory");
+
 static void dwc2_set_esp32s31_params(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)
 {
 	struct dwc2_core_params *p = &hsotg->params;
 
+	/*
+	 * Say which side of the coherency question this device landed on.
+	 * "dma_alloc_coherent returns cached memory here" has been repeated in
+	 * this project's notes for a long time without anyone checking it, and
+	 * descriptor DMA is exactly where it would bite.
+	 */
+	dev_info(hsotg->dev, "DMA is %s per the device tree\n",
+		 hsotg->dev->dma_coherent ? "COHERENT (descriptors cached)"
+					  : "non-coherent (descriptors uncached)");
+
 	p->phy_type = DWC2_PHY_TYPE_PARAM_UTMI;
 	p->phy_utmi_width = 16;
 	p->host_dma = true;
@@ -94,8 +110,25 @@
 	 * by about 15x, taking the controller's cost with an idle keyboard
 	 * from ~7% of this core to under 1%.
 	 */
-	p->dma_desc_enable = true;
-	p->dma_desc_fs_enable = true;
+	/*
+	 * Runtime toggle, because descriptor DMA is the leading suspect for
+	 * keystrokes that go missing for a few seconds and then come back.
+	 *
+	 * The descriptor lists live in dma_alloc_coherent() memory, and on this
+	 * SoC that returns CACHED memory - the same trap that bit the AXI GDMA
+	 * and the PPA, both of which had to be pointed at an uncached alias
+	 * before their engines would read what the CPU had written. If the
+	 * controller and the CPU disagree about a descriptor here, periodic IN
+	 * transfers simply stop being collected until something evicts the
+	 * line, which is exactly what "it works, then stops, then works again"
+	 * looks like from the keyboard.
+	 *
+	 * Turning it off costs CPU (the comment below is why it was turned on)
+	 * but proves or disproves the theory in one reboot instead of a
+	 * rebuild per hypothesis.
+	 */
+	p->dma_desc_enable = desc_dma;
+	p->dma_desc_fs_enable = desc_dma;
 	/* ESP-IDF uses SINGLE transfers when enabling this DWC2 AHB master. */
 	p->ahbcfg = GAHBCFG_HBSTLEN_SINGLE << GAHBCFG_HBSTLEN_SHIFT;
 	/*
--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/espressif/esp32s31.dtsi
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/espressif/esp32s31.dtsi
@@ -938,6 +938,30 @@
 			phys = <&usb_phy>;
 			phy-names = "usb2-phy";
 			dr_mode = "host";
+			/*
+			 * riscv selects ARCH_DMA_DEFAULT_COHERENT, so with neither
+			 * property present of_dma_is_coherent() returns true and
+			 * this controller is treated as cache coherent. That makes
+			 * dma_alloc_coherent() hand back an ordinary CACHED linear
+			 * mapping instead of taking the DMA_DIRECT_REMAP path.
+			 *
+			 * dwc2 keeps its descriptor lists and frame list in that
+			 * memory and the controller reads them by DMA, so any line
+			 * still dirty in the CPU's cache is invisible to it. That
+			 * is the same failure the PPA hit, where the engine read a
+			 * stale descriptor and never started, and it was fixed
+			 * there by hand with an uncached alias.
+			 *
+			 * Saying so in the DT fixes it in the right place: the
+			 * coherent allocator returns an uncached mapping and every
+			 * driver on this node gets it, with no per-driver aliasing.
+			 *
+			 * Streaming maps are unaffected in practice - this hart has
+			 * no zicbom and nothing registers riscv_nonstd_cache_ops,
+			 * so arch_sync_dma_for_device() patches to a nop either
+			 * way. Only the coherent allocation changes.
+			 */
+			dma-noncoherent;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
--- a/arch/riscv/configs/esp32s31_defconfig
+++ b/arch/riscv/configs/esp32s31_defconfig
@@ -109,7 +109,7 @@
 CONFIG_FPU=y
 
 # Kernel command line
-CONFIG_CMDLINE="dw_mmc.lost_irq_poll=0 dw_mmc.low_pwr=0 console=tty0 console=ttyS0,1000000n8 root=mtd:rootfs rootfstype=cramfs ro init=/init"
+CONFIG_CMDLINE="dw_mmc.lost_irq_poll=0 dw_mmc.low_pwr=0 console=tty0 console=ttyS0,1000000n8 root=mtd:rootfs rootfstype=cramfs ro init=/init usbhid.quirks=0x2dc8:0x5201:0x00000400"
 CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE=y
 
 # Built-in DTB (no external loader needed)
